The coordinator had it wrong. It wrote that October's sections were "already by subject" and never asked how they came to be so. The curator's words of 2026-09-30 name the step: "let a fable worker reorder them in order for them to be grouped by topics".

A Sonnet scout is digging it up now, from git and the transcripts:

- each time FACTS or POSITIONS was regrouped by topic: the commit, the worker and its brief, word for word;
- how an entry was moved, split or merged, and what check showed that nothing was lost;
- how the chunks of 2026-10-13 were cut from the sections;
- how many scouts and writers ran side by side on 2026-10-13 and on 2026-10-22.

On the parallel run, what the skill's part on delegation holds today, from October: four or five workflow runs at once, eight or nine agents. Each run gets its share of the chunks. Each chunk writes only into its own folder. Only the coordinator writes the record, so two runs never write the same lines. A chunk that splits or merges an entry that another chunk also changes is applied first. The scout checks this against what really ran.

On the page: section 5, part E no longer says that the sorting is new. It says that the step existed and is being looked up, and it is rewritten when the scout reports. Part B, step 3 now holds how the chunks run side by side.

